#!/bin/sh
# CMake hands the compiler's absolute path; distccd refuses one ("compiler name
# cannot be an absolute path"). distcc resolves a bare name through the server's
# /usr/lib/distcc whitelist, which works, so drop the path and keep the name.
#
# PATH must hold the real toolchain: distcc needs it for the local fallback, and
# masquerade mode must not find this script again.
compiler=$1
shift
PATH="/opt/devkitpro/devkitA64/bin:$PATH"
export PATH
# A precompiled header is never sent away: distcc compiles from preprocessed
# text, and a .gch built from that has every declaration but none of the
# include guards, so each unit that later includes a header normally redefines
# the C library ("redefinition of std::__terminate").
for arg in "$@"; do
    case $arg in *.gch|c++-header|c-header) exec "$compiler" "$@" ;; esac
done

# A helper that has gone (a phone asleep, off the network) must not fail the
# build: distcc's own fallback re-runs a precompiled-header unit locally in a
# way that redefines the C library, and every such unit fails. With no helper
# answering, compile here directly instead.
for host in ${DISTCC_HOSTS:-}; do
    case $host in --*) continue ;; esac
    address=${host%%/*}
    name=${address%%:*}
    port=${address#*:}
    [ "$port" = "$address" ] && port=3632
    if timeout 2 bash -c "exec 3<>/dev/tcp/$name/$port" 2>/dev/null; then
        exec /usr/bin/distcc "$(basename "$compiler")" "$@"
    fi
done
exec "$compiler" "$@"
